function G(id,topic,slug,title,summary,answer,answerSources,sections,related,service){
 return {id,topic,slug,path:'/guides/'+slug,title,summary,answer,answerSources,sections,
  sources:[...new Set([...answerSources,...sections.flatMap(s=>s[2])])],related,service};
}
export const expansionGuides=[
 G('T05','testosterone','trt-monitoring-and-follow-up',
 'TRT follow-up: monitoring, reviews and changing provider',
 'Understand the ongoing care arrangement, the questions a review should cover and what to organise before moving provider.',
 'TRT needs ongoing clinical review, including whether it is helping and whether unwanted effects or monitoring results change the plan. Ask who owns each part of your care. If moving provider, arrange the handover before relying on a new prescription; do not change treatment yourself.', ['S03','S20'],[
  ['Follow-up is part of treatment','A prescription is the beginning of an ongoing arrangement. The Endocrine Society recommends reviewing response, adverse effects and adherence after treatment starts. Monitoring may include testosterone and blood count, with prostate assessment where appropriate. The responsible clinician sets the tests and timing for your circumstances; this page is not a monitoring schedule.',['S03','S20']],
  ['Keep a useful record','Take a short account of what has changed since the previous appointment. Separate symptoms, everyday function and unwanted effects. For example, “I am less tired at work, but have noticed this new problem” gives the reviewer something concrete to discuss. Bring the current prescription details and copies of results rather than remembered numbers.',[]],
  ['Know who does what','Ask who requests each blood test, reads the result, contacts you and decides whether another appointment is needed. Find out how to report a problem between routine reviews. If different organisations provide testing and prescribing, ask how information reaches the prescriber. Do not assume that a purchased laboratory report includes a clinical review.',[]],
  ['Before changing provider','Ask the proposed provider what records and reassessment they require, whether they will accept responsibility, and when that decision will be made. Gather the original assessment, prescription history, monitoring results and recent review letters. Discuss continuity with the current prescriber. A new account or paid consultation does not by itself confirm that prescribing will transfer.',[]],
  ['Compare the whole arrangement','Request a written explanation of what follow-up costs cover, how extra tests are charged and what happens if treatment is unsuitable or stopped by the clinician. Mention changes in fertility plans at reviews. A useful next step is to write down the name of the person responsible for the next result and how you will hear from them.',['S20']]
 ],['T02','T03','T06'],'/shift-health/trt-explained'),
 G('T08','testosterone','low-sex-drive-and-erection-changes',
 'Lower sex drive or erection changes: where to start',
 'A broader starting point for changes in desire or erections, including what to tell a GP and why a hormone test is only one possibility.',
 'Changes in desire and difficulty getting or keeping an erection are different concerns, and both can have several causes. They do not automatically mean low testosterone. Speak to a GP or sexual health service if erection problems keep happening, or to a GP if low desire worries you.', ['S34','S35'],[
  ['Describe the change in your own words','It may be less interest in sex, an erection that does not last, or both. Say when it began, whether it is occasional or persistent and what is bothering you most. You do not need a medical label before asking for help. A clinician can ask the further questions needed to understand the pattern.',[]],
  ['Keep more than hormones in view','The NHS describes several possible contributors, including stress, relationship difficulties, medicines, alcohol and physical health conditions. Recurrent erection problems can warrant a wider health assessment. Testing testosterone may be appropriate in some circumstances, but choosing it first from a shopping page can leave the original concern insufficiently explored.',['S34','S35']],
  ['Prepare for the conversation','Bring an accurate list of prescribed medicines, non-prescription treatments, supplements and any hormones used. Note changes in sleep, mood or health and whether future children matter to you. You can ask for a private conversation, explain that a topic feels awkward, or bring notes so you do not have to remember everything under pressure.',[]],
  ['Agree a route that fits the cause','Help may involve looking at a condition, reviewing medicines or supporting emotional and relationship concerns. Treatment depends on the assessment. Do not stop a prescription because its leaflet mentions sexual side effects, or assume that an online hormone or erection product is suitable. Ask the prescriber or pharmacist about your particular medicines.',['S34','S35']],
  ['One useful next step','If the problem is recurring or worrying you, book an appropriate appointment and write one sentence about the main change you want help with. Ask what assessment is proposed, what it can establish and how follow-up works. Ask Timber can explain general terms and help organise questions; the individual assessment belongs with a healthcare professional.',[]]
 ],['T01','T02','T03'],'/shift-health/erectile-dysfunction'),
 G('B03','bloods','hba1c-and-blood-glucose',
 'HbA1c and blood glucose: what is the difference?',
 'Understand two different ways of looking at blood sugar, without turning a home reading or laboratory number into a diagnosis.',
 'HbA1c reflects average blood glucose over roughly the previous two to three months. A glucose measurement describes the level at the time the sample is taken. They answer different questions and are not interchangeable; the clinician arranging testing should explain which is appropriate and how the result will be followed up.', ['S22','S23'],[
  ['A longer view and a moment in time','HbA1c measures glucose attached to haemoglobin in red blood cells. It is useful for particular diabetes assessments and monitoring. A glucose test is more immediate and its interpretation depends on the situation, including whether it was fasting or taken at another time. Neither result tells the whole story about a person’s health.',['S22','S23']],
  ['Preparation depends on the actual test','HbA1c alone does not generally require fasting. Other tests collected at the same appointment may have preparation instructions. Follow the booking information and ask the provider if anything is unclear. Do not skip food or change diabetes medicines on the assumption that every blood sugar test has the same preparation.',['S04','S23']],
  ['Know which measurement is on the report','Keep the test name, date, units and laboratory information together. HbA1c can be reported in different units from a glucose reading, so numbers found online may not be directly comparable. Before trying to make sense of a result, confirm what was measured and why. Ask for an explanation rather than converting a number into a diagnosis yourself.',[]],
  ['Why an individual result needs context','HbA1c is not appropriate for every diagnostic situation, and some conditions affecting blood or haemoglobin can affect interpretation. An apparently reassuring result should not end a conversation about ongoing symptoms. A clinician may choose another measurement or arrange confirmation, depending on the question being investigated.',['S23']],
  ['Get a clear follow-up plan','Ask: Is this a screening, diagnostic or monitoring test? Who interprets it? When will I hear? What should I do if symptoms continue before then? If you already have diabetes, use your agreed care plan and contact your care team about concerns. An educational guide cannot set your targets or advise changes to treatment.',[]]
 ],['B01','B02','C01'],'/shift-health/blood-tests'),
 G('B05','bloods','iron-ferritin-and-full-blood-count',
 'Iron, ferritin and full blood count: different pieces of the picture',
 'Separate blood count, iron stores and symptoms, and understand why the cause matters before starting a supplement.',
 'A full blood count and ferritin are different tests. A blood count includes information about blood cells; ferritin helps assess iron stores but can also rise with inflammation. Tiredness or one flagged result is not enough to choose iron treatment. Ask the clinician to explain the findings and their possible cause.', ['S24','S25'],[
  ['What a full blood count contributes','A full blood count, often shortened to FBC, includes haemoglobin and information about different blood cells. It can help investigate anaemia, but it does not identify every cause of tiredness. If the report includes several flagged lines, keep them together for the reviewer rather than treating each one as a separate diagnosis.',['S24']],
  ['What ferritin adds','Ferritin is used as an indicator of stored iron. It is also affected by inflammation, so a value within or above a reference range does not always settle the question of iron deficiency. The laboratory context and the reason for testing matter. A home threshold indicator is not the same as this fuller assessment.',['S25']],
  ['Ask about the reason, not just replacement','If iron deficiency anaemia is diagnosed, treatment and investigation of the cause belong together. Possible explanations need assessment, including whether blood loss is involved. Buying iron because you feel drained can leave the original problem unexplored. Report relevant symptoms to the GP instead of assuming that diet is the only explanation.',['S24']],
  ['Make the appointment easier','Bring the complete report, previous results if available, and a list of medicines and supplements. Describe tiredness in practical terms: what you could usually do, what has changed and when. Mention any bleeding or bowel changes. You can ask what is known already, what still needs checking and who will arrange the next step.',[]],
  ['Avoid a test-to-product shortcut','Ask whether treatment is needed, how its effect will be checked and what to do if it is difficult to tolerate. A supplement label does not establish that iron is appropriate for you. Keep iron products securely away from children. Do not copy another person’s dose or assume a general multivitamin can treat diagnosed anaemia.',['S48']]
 ],['B01','F06','C06'],'/shift-health/blood-tests'),
 G('B06','bloods','thyroid-tests-tsh-and-t4',
 'Thyroid tests: TSH, thyroid hormones and symptoms',
 'Understand the difference between a focused TSH check and a clinical thyroid assessment, and prepare for a useful follow-up.',
 'TSH and thyroid hormones such as T4 help clinicians assess thyroid function. A focused TSH home test does not provide the same information as a clinical assessment. Symptoms, the exact test and other circumstances matter; ask the professional arranging testing to explain the result and any follow-up.', ['S26'],[
  ['The names on the report','TSH stands for thyroid-stimulating hormone. T4, or thyroxine, is one of the hormones made by the thyroid. A clinician may use these measurements together when investigating an underactive thyroid. A kit that indicates whether TSH crosses a particular threshold should not be described as a complete picture of thyroid function.',['S26']],
  ['Symptoms can overlap','Tiredness, feeling cold and changes in weight are among symptoms associated with an underactive thyroid, but such symptoms can have other explanations. It is reasonable to ask about them without deciding the cause first. A symptom checklist or a reassuring home result cannot replace an assessment when you continue to feel unwell.',['S26']],
  ['Check what the service actually includes','Read whether the purchase is a self-read indicator or a sample sent to a laboratory. Look for the exact measurements, how results are presented and whether a healthcare professional will interpret them. “Thyroid test” is a broad label. Before paying, ask what question this particular test is designed to answer and what happens afterwards.',[]],
  ['Bring the relevant information','Take the full report and a current medicines and supplements list. If you already receive thyroid treatment, ask your own care team how any proposed extra test fits the existing plan. Follow the preparation instructions they provide. Do not stop prescribed treatment or change its timing simply to match advice about someone else’s test online.',[]],
  ['Use the result as part of a conversation','Ask what the result can establish, whether additional testing is needed and how you will be contacted. If symptoms continue, say so even if the report looks reassuring. Ask Timber can explain what TSH or T4 means in general and help prepare these questions. It cannot diagnose a thyroid condition, interpret your own numbers or recommend a treatment dose.',[]]
 ],['B01','B02','C03'],'/shift-health/blood-tests'),
 G('B07','bloods','liver-and-kidney-blood-tests',
 'Liver and kidney blood tests: what they can and cannot show',
 'Understand the purpose of these groups of tests and why a blood panel, urine test and clinical assessment have different roles.',
 'Liver blood tests measure several substances that need to be considered together and in context. Kidney assessment often combines blood-based measures, such as creatinine and estimated filtration, with urine testing. A normal-looking panel is not a general certificate of health, and a flagged value needs an appropriate clinical explanation.', ['S27','S28'],[
  ['A group of liver markers','A liver panel may include measurements such as ALT, ALP and bilirubin. Despite the familiar label “liver function tests”, these do not all measure the same aspect of liver health. NHS Specialist Pharmacy Service emphasises that no single blood test gives a complete assessment; symptoms, history and the pattern of results matter.',['S27']],
  ['Kidney blood measurements','Creatinine is used to estimate how well the kidneys filter blood, commonly reported as eGFR. An estimate needs clinical interpretation rather than a comparison with a stranger’s number online. Kidney assessment may also involve testing urine for albumin. A urine indicator and a blood-based estimate answer different questions and do not substitute for each other.',['S28']],
  ['Keep the reason for testing visible','You may be having a test to investigate symptoms, monitor a known condition or check something during treatment. Tell the reviewer which applies. Keep the complete report, not just the highlighted lines, and bring previous results if available. Ask whether the pattern or change over time matters more than one isolated measurement.',[]],
  ['Do not turn a flag into a cleanse','A result cannot tell you to buy a detox product, stop a medicine or start a restrictive diet. Bring your medicine and supplement list to the clinician who reviews it, including non-prescription products. If a provider sells both testing and supplements, you can ask for a clear clinical explanation before considering any purchase.',[]],
  ['Leave with a plan you understand','Ask which findings need attention, whether any repeat or different assessment is needed and who will arrange it. Ask how to seek help if symptoms develop while waiting. Persistent or worsening symptoms deserve medical advice even when a previous panel was within range. Ask Timber can explain general terminology; personal results and treatment decisions require your healthcare professional.',[]]
 ],['B01','C07','F02'],'/shift-health/blood-tests'),
 G('B08','bloods','vitamin-d-and-b12-testing',
 'Vitamin D and B12 testing: when might a check be useful?',
 'Understand why a clinician might investigate a deficiency, how testing differs from routine seasonal advice and what to ask next.',
 'Vitamin testing is most useful when it answers a particular clinical question. B12 assessment involves symptoms and blood findings, while routine UK vitamin D advice does not mean everyone needs a blood test. Ask why a test is being proposed, how it would change care and who will explain the result.', ['S29','S30'],[
  ['Start with the question','“I am tired, so which vitamins should I test?” is an understandable question, but tiredness has many possible explanations. Describe the pattern and any other symptoms to a GP. The useful decision is which assessment could explain the problem, rather than which package contains the most vitamin measurements. A bigger panel is not automatically a better answer.',['S01']],
  ['B12 is more than one number','The NHS explains that B12 or folate deficiency assessment uses symptoms and blood test information. A laboratory number alone may not settle the question. The cause also matters when deciding treatment. If symptoms persist, discuss them with the clinician rather than using a reference range as a reason to dismiss the concern.',['S29']],
  ['Vitamin D advice and deficiency treatment differ','NHS autumn and winter advice is general public health guidance. It is separate from investigating symptoms or treating a confirmed deficiency. Do not assume a commercial test is a necessary first step for everyone following seasonal advice. If a clinician has already given you a treatment plan, discuss questions with them instead of replacing it with a general supplement recommendation.',['S30']],
  ['Make a complete list','Tell the professional about your diet, relevant health history and every supplement you already use, including combination products. Bring the actual labels or photographs so the ingredients can be checked accurately. Ask the provider whether anything affects preparation or interpretation. Do not stop medicines or prescribed supplements solely because you have booked a test.',[]],
  ['Know what happens after the result','Before paying, ask who reviews the report, what the service can and cannot establish, and whether follow-up is included. If a result needs treatment, ask how the cause and response will be assessed. Ask Timber can explain the difference between a nutrient, a test and a reference range; it cannot diagnose deficiency or choose a personal replacement dose.',[]]
 ],['B01','F05','F06'],'/shift-health/blood-tests')
];

expansionGuides.push(
 G('C03','testing','home-test-accuracy-and-false-results',
 'How reliable are home tests? False results and useful questions',
 'Read an accuracy claim properly and understand why the exact test, intended use and follow-up matter.',
 'There is no single accuracy percentage for all home tests. Reliability depends on the particular kit, what it measures, how it is used and who it was studied in. A reassuring result does not explain persistent symptoms; an unexpected result may need clinical follow-up rather than an immediate diagnosis.', ['S31'],[
  ['Find out what “accurate” means','A 2025 BMJ review of UK consumer self-tests identified gaps in available evidence and clarity of information. It reviewed products sold in 2023, so it does not establish the performance of every kit sold today. Its practical lesson is to ask for evidence about the exact product and intended use, rather than accepting a headline percentage.',['S31']],
  ['Different errors mean different things','A false positive suggests a condition or finding that is not actually present; a false negative misses one that is present. Sensitivity describes how well a test identifies people who have the condition, while specificity concerns correctly identifying those who do not. Neither word means a test gives certainty for every individual.',['S31']],
  ['Match the claim to the question','Ask whether the study used the same sample type, instructions and population as the product being offered. Check whether the result is an exact laboratory measurement or an indication above or below a threshold. A result about one marker cannot automatically answer a broader question such as “Am I fertile?” or “Are my kidneys healthy?”.',[]],
  ['Collection and reading are part of the process','Before using a kit, check the expiry date, storage requirements, sample instructions and reading window in its own leaflet. If you cannot complete a step, ask the provider rather than improvising. Keep the leaflet and report together. For a posted sample, confirm how to package it and when the laboratory can receive it.',[]],
  ['Plan the next step before buying','Ask who can explain an unclear or unexpected result and whether further assessment costs extra. Seek medical advice for persistent symptoms, even if a home result appears reassuring. If you already need a GP assessment, a kit should not become a condition you impose on yourself before booking. Ask Timber can explain test terminology, but it cannot validate your result or diagnose from a photograph.',[]]
 ],['C01','C08','B01'],'/shift-health/testing'),
 G('C04','testing','preparing-and-posting-a-test-sample',
 'Preparing a sample: fasting, timing, medicines and posting',
 'A practical preparation checklist, with the exact kit and clinical instructions kept in charge.',
 'Preparation depends on the test and collection method. Use the instructions supplied by the provider; fasting is only needed for some blood tests. Ask before changing medicines, food or timing. If the instructions are missing or contradictory, resolve that with the provider before collecting the sample.', ['S04'],[
  ['Read the whole process first','Open the instructions before the day you intend to collect. Check that the kit is complete and that you understand the sample, labelling, packaging and return steps. Keep the provider’s contact details nearby. If a step is unclear, resolve it while you still have time; a rushed collection is a poor moment to discover a missing component.',[]],
  ['Fasting is test-specific','The NHS explains that some blood tests need preparation such as fasting, while many do not. If you are having several tests together, ask which instructions apply to the whole appointment. Do not assume that “morning test” means “no food”, or decide to fast for longer in the hope of improving accuracy.',['S04']],
  ['Ask about medicines and supplements','Give the provider an accurate list and ask whether anything affects this particular test. A clinician may give specific directions, but do not stop or adjust prescribed treatment yourself. If you have a condition that makes fasting or collection difficult, raise that before the appointment. Ask for instructions you can refer back to rather than relying on memory.',['S04']],
  ['Check timing and return arrangements','Some tests have particular collection or delivery requirements. Ask which days you can collect, what happens around weekends or bank holidays, and how quickly the sample must arrive. Follow the supplied packaging and transport instructions. A general guide cannot supply one safe timetable for a blood tube, semen sample and urine sample.',[]],
  ['If something goes wrong','Tell the provider what happened: for example, a missed preparation step, incomplete sample, labelling error or delayed return. Ask whether the sample can still be used and what the replacement process is. Do not alter a label, top up a tube or send a questionable sample without advice. Record when you should expect a result and who to contact if it does not arrive.',[]]
 ],['C01','C05','C08'],'/shift-health/testing'),
 G('C05','testing','blood-sample-collection-methods',
 'Finger-prick, upper-arm and venous collection compared',
 'Understand collection options and the questions to ask about suitability, support and the exact panel.',
 'Finger-prick and some upper-arm devices collect small blood samples; venous collection takes blood from a vein. The right method depends on the test and the laboratory’s accepted sample. A convenient collection device does not establish that every panel is supported or that all methods are interchangeable.', ['S04'],[
  ['Collection and analysis are separate','A sample collected at home may still be analysed in a laboratory. A clinic-collected sample may go to the same kind of laboratory. Where collection happens does not, by itself, tell you which measurements are included, what quality checks apply or who interprets the report. Start by checking the exact panel and accepted method.',[]],
  ['Finger-prick collection','A finger-prick kit is intended to collect a small sample using the supplied equipment. Follow its own instructions and ask the provider what to do if collection is difficult. Do not combine instructions from another kit or improvise to fill a tube. Ask about replacement arrangements and whether another supported collection option is available.',[]],
  ['Upper-arm devices','Some devices collect a small blood sample from the upper arm. Their design and instructions vary, so read the information for the named device and panel. Check whether that method is available for the service you are considering now. A trial or proposed collection option should not be treated as a confirmed booking route or proof of suitability for every test.',[]],
  ['Venous collection','A trained professional takes venous blood from a vein, usually in the arm. The NHS describes the usual blood-test process and practical considerations. Tell the professional if you are worried about needles or have previously felt faint. Ask the service where collection takes place, whether it is included in the price and what information you should bring.',['S04']],
  ['Compare the whole process','Ask which methods the laboratory accepts for your panel, how an inadequate sample is handled and who pays for a repeat if one is needed. Consider collection support, travel, return timing and the clinical explanation of results. Do not choose solely by a promise of convenience. If symptoms need assessment, discuss them with a healthcare professional before selecting a commercial panel.',[]]
 ],['C01','C04','B02'],'/shift-health/blood-tests'),
 G('C06','testing','fit-bowel-tests-and-symptoms',
 'Bowel FIT testing: what it checks and when symptoms need a GP',
 'Separate screening, private home testing and investigation of symptoms, with a clear route for medical help.',
 'FIT looks for blood in a poo sample. It is used in bowel screening and can also form part of investigating symptoms. A positive result does not by itself diagnose cancer, and a negative result does not rule it out. Speak to a GP about bowel symptoms rather than waiting for a home kit.', ['S32','S33'],[
  ['What FIT measures','FIT means faecal immunochemical test. It checks for blood that may not be visible. A finding of blood needs the appropriate explanation and follow-up; there can be causes other than cancer. The test is one step in a pathway, not a complete look inside the bowel or a general cancer check.',['S32']],
  ['Screening and symptoms are different routes','NHS screening aims to find problems before symptoms. Invitations and arrangements depend on the national programme. A shop-bought test does not enrol you in that programme or automatically arrange its follow-up. If symptoms develop, discuss them with a GP even if a previous screening or home result was reassuring.',['S32']],
  ['Make the important call','Ask a GP about blood in poo, a continuing change in bowel habit or other concerning bowel symptoms. NHS advice is to seek urgent GP help or NHS 111 for black or dark red poo or bloody diarrhoea. For non-stop bleeding or a large amount of blood or large clots, call 999 or go to A&E.',['S33']],
  ['Check the follow-up before purchasing','Ask whether the particular product is read at home or processed by a laboratory, what its result categories mean and who will explain them. Find out how an unclear result is handled. A private result should not leave you guessing whether someone has arranged further assessment; ask directly who is responsible and what you need to do.',[]],
  ['Prepare a useful description','Write down when symptoms started, what changed, any medicines and relevant family history to discuss with the GP. Bring the result and leaflet if you have already used a kit. You do not need to prove that something is wrong before asking for help. Ask Timber can explain FIT in general; it cannot rule out bowel cancer or decide what your own result means.',[]]
 ],['C03','C08','B05'],'/shift-health/testing'),
 G('C07','testing','urine-albumin-and-kidney-tests',
 'Urine albumin tests and kidney health: understanding the limits',
 'Understand why a urine indicator, laboratory ACR and kidney blood test answer different questions.',
 'Albumin in urine can help clinicians assess kidney health, but a home indicator is not a complete kidney assessment. Laboratory urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, often called ACR, and blood-based estimated filtration have different roles. Discuss symptoms or an unexpected result with a clinician rather than treating one test as a verdict.', ['S28'],[
  ['What the urine test is looking for','Albumin is a protein. Testing urine for albumin helps assess whether protein is leaking into urine. The NHS describes urine testing alongside blood tests in the investigation of chronic kidney disease. A laboratory ACR measurement relates albumin to creatinine in the urine; it is not simply another name for blood creatinine.',['S28']],
  ['Know the product’s scope','Check whether the test gives a threshold indication, a broader strip reading or a laboratory ACR result. Do not translate one type of result into another using a table found online. Ask what the particular kit has been designed to detect, how the result should be read and which situations need professional follow-up.',[]],
  ['Blood testing contributes something different','A blood creatinine measurement can be used to estimate filtration, reported as eGFR. That is a different question from whether albumin is present in urine. A reassuring result from one does not automatically settle the other. The clinician chooses and interprets the appropriate combination in light of history and symptoms.',['S28']],
  ['Prepare and collect as instructed','Use the exact sample instructions for the kit or laboratory. Ask about timing, containers, medicines and any circumstances the provider needs to know. If you cannot complete the collection correctly, contact them before sending it. Keep the original result, including units or category, so the professional reviewing it can see what was actually measured.',[]],
  ['Make follow-up explicit','Ask who will explain an unexpected finding and whether repeat or different testing may be needed. If you have an existing kidney condition, discuss additional testing with your care team so it fits the agreed plan. Persistent or worsening symptoms need medical advice regardless of a home result. Ask Timber can explain ACR and eGFR generally; it cannot diagnose kidney disease or assess personal numbers.',[]]
 ],['B07','B01','C03'],'/shift-health/testing'),
 G('C08','testing','invalid-or-unclear-home-test-result',
 'Invalid, unclear or unexpected home result: what happens next?',
 'A practical way to handle a result you cannot use, without guessing, repeatedly buying kits or delaying assessment.',
 'An invalid result is not a negative result. Use the particular kit’s instructions and contact its provider about an unreadable or failed test. For an unexpected result or continuing symptoms, seek appropriate medical advice. Do not diagnose from a line photograph or keep repeating tests until one gives the answer you hoped for.', ['S31'],[
  ['Keep different situations separate','“Invalid” means the test has not supplied a usable result under its instructions. “Unexpected” means it has supplied a result you did not anticipate. “Unclear” might mean the reading or explanation is hard to understand. These need different kinds of help. Start by recording the wording used by the kit or laboratory rather than assigning your own category.',[]],
  ['Return to the exact instructions','Find the sections on the control indicator, reading time and invalid results. Do not use another brand’s examples to decide what your kit means. Keep the packaging and batch details if you need to contact the supplier. If the instructions do not resolve the problem, ask the provider to explain the correct next step for that product.',[]],
  ['Ask about the process, not a guarantee','Tell the provider when the sample was collected and read, and describe any difficulty honestly. Ask whether a repeat, replacement or different collection method is needed and what charges apply. A photograph may help their product-support team understand a technical issue; it should not be treated as a clinical diagnosis from Ask Timber.',[]],
  ['A result does not replace assessment','UK research on self-tests highlights the importance of clear evidence and understandable instructions. A kit’s result category cannot by itself explain all symptoms or guarantee that a condition is absent. If you feel unwell, are worried about a finding or already need a clinical review, arrange that conversation rather than waiting to complete another purchase.',['S31']],
  ['Bring useful information to follow-up','Keep the result, leaflet, test name and sample date together. Explain why you tested and what symptoms prompted it. Ask the healthcare professional what this particular result can establish and what remains to be checked. For urgent symptoms use urgent medical services; a slow supplier response is not a reason to postpone care. You can also ask for help understanding general test language before that appointment.',[]]
 ],['C03','C04','B01'],'/shift-health/testing')
);

expansionGuides.push(
 G('D03','fertility','semen-analysis-concentration-movement-shape',
 'Semen analysis explained: concentration, movement and shape',
 'Understand what a laboratory semen analysis contributes and how it differs from a home concentration check or testosterone test.',
 'A laboratory semen analysis looks at several features, including sperm concentration, movement and shape. These findings help a fertility assessment but cannot guarantee or rule out pregnancy on their own. A home concentration-only test and a testosterone blood test do not provide the same information.', ['S06','S21'],[
  ['Three different features','Concentration concerns the number of sperm in a volume of semen. Motility describes movement; morphology concerns shape. A laboratory analysis may report additional measurements. A single “count” label can hide these distinctions, so ask what the particular test includes rather than assuming that all sperm tests assess the same things.',['S06','S21']],
  ['The report belongs in a wider assessment','The findings help the clinical team decide what to investigate or discuss next. Fertility involves more than one laboratory result, and assessment may involve both partners. Avoid reading one number as a score of masculinity or a prediction of whether a family is possible. Ask the professional to explain the complete report in context.',['S13']],
  ['Collection instructions matter','The clinic or laboratory should explain preparation, the container, collection and delivery requirements. Use those instructions rather than borrowing rules from a home kit. Ask before the appointment if you expect difficulty providing the sample or need privacy or accessibility support. Tell the team if collection was incomplete or any instruction could not be followed.',[]],
  ['Why another sample may be discussed','A clinician may arrange repeat analysis when a finding needs confirmation. NICE guidance covers repeat testing within the assessment pathway; the team decides the appropriate timing and whether further investigation is needed. Repeating a different retail kit is not automatically equivalent. Ask what a repeat is intended to clarify and who will discuss the results.',['S21']],
  ['Useful questions for the appointment','Ask: Which features were measured? What can this result explain? Is further assessment needed? Does previous treatment or hormone use matter? What happens next for everyone involved? Bring the report and medicines list. Ask Timber can explain general terms and help organise these questions, but cannot interpret your personal semen analysis or recommend a fertility treatment.',[]]
 ],['D01','D02','T02'],'/shift-health/fertility'),
 G('D04','fertility','planning-a-family-and-everyday-health',
 'Planning a family: everyday health and realistic expectations',
 'Make useful changes around food, smoking, alcohol and sleep without treating pregnancy as a reward for a perfect routine.',
 'Looking after everyday health is worthwhile when planning a family, but no food, supplement or routine can guarantee pregnancy. Ask for a fertility assessment at the appropriate time rather than waiting to complete a lifestyle programme. Support and clinical assessment can happen alongside practical changes.', ['S06','S13'],[
  ['Start with changes you can sustain','The NHS includes smoking, alcohol and other health factors in advice around low sperm count. Treat this as a reason to discuss your circumstances, not as proof that a difficulty conceiving is your fault. Choose manageable changes and ask for support where needed. A demanding plan that adds pressure is not the only way forward.',['S06']],
  ['Give ordinary meals a place','Plan food around regular, practical meals that you can afford and repeat. Write down the obstacles first: long shifts, limited cooking space, rushed lunches or reduced appetite. Ask for appropriate nutrition support if those are difficult to manage. There is no need to build a special “fertility diet” around a powder or expensive ingredient before you can seek help.',['S17']],
  ['Make the plan fit real life','Pick one change that solves an actual problem, such as having a lunch ready for a busy day or setting aside time to talk without test results on the table. If you share family planning with a partner, agree what feels useful to both of you. You do not need to monitor every meal, night or activity to earn an assessment.',[]],
  ['Be cautious about promises','Ask what evidence supports a product’s claim, whether it studied the exact product and whether it measured an outcome that matters to you. A change in a laboratory marker is not automatically proof of improved chances of pregnancy. Do not treat a supplement bundle as a substitute for identifying the cause of difficulty conceiving or reviewing medicines.',[]],
  ['Keep the assessment moving','Follow NHS advice on when to ask a GP for fertility help, including circumstances where earlier advice is appropriate. Tell the clinician about medicines, hormone use and relevant history while making any everyday changes you choose. Ask what happens next and how long the assessment may take. Seek emotional support if the process is affecting your wellbeing or relationship.',['S13','S38']]
 ],['D01','D05','D08'],'/shift-health/fertility'),
 G('D05','fertility','fertility-and-your-medicines-list',
 'Fertility and medicines: what to tell your GP or specialist',
 'Prepare an accurate treatment history and a better conversation about suitability, without stopping or swapping medicines yourself.',
 'Some medicines and non-prescribed hormones can affect fertility. Give the clinician a complete current and past treatment list, including supplements and anabolic steroids. Do not stop a prescribed medicine on your own: discuss the possible effects, alternatives and timing with the responsible prescriber or fertility team.', ['S36','S03'],[
  ['Make the list complete','Include prescribed medicines, pharmacy products, supplements, injections, gels and anything bought online. Record the name, what you use it for and when you started or stopped, as accurately as you can. Bring packaging or photographs if the name is uncertain. An honest list is more useful than leaving out something because it feels awkward to mention.',[]],
  ['Explain why you are asking now','Say whether you are planning a family, currently trying or already having an assessment. Mention previous fertility investigations and any future plans that could affect treatment choices. The NHS identifies medicines and drug use among possible contributors to infertility, but that does not tell you whether a particular medicine explains your own situation.',['S36']],
  ['Testosterone deserves a clear conversation','Externally supplied testosterone can reduce sperm production. The Endocrine Society advises against starting testosterone treatment when fertility is planned in the near term. If you already use TRT, contact the responsible prescriber about your plans. Do not add online hormones or copy a recovery regimen while waiting for that discussion.',['S03']],
  ['Ask about decisions and responsibility','Useful questions include: Could this treatment matter for our plans? Who should review it? Are there alternatives suitable for my condition? Is specialist input needed? Who coordinates any change? Ask for an explanation you can take away. A general list of medicines associated with fertility effects cannot decide which option is safest for an individual.',[]],
  ['Do not make a solo stopping plan','A medicine may be treating an important condition, and changing it needs individual advice. If different teams are involved, ask how the prescriber, GP and fertility service will share information. Keep a record of agreed next steps and the person to contact. Ask Timber can help organise your questions and explain general terms, but cannot check personal suitability or direct treatment changes.',[]]
 ],['T02','D01','D07'],'/shift-health/fertility'),
 G('D06','fertility','post-vasectomy-testing-and-clearance',
 'After a vasectomy: why the correct follow-up test matters',
 'Understand the difference between post-vasectomy clearance and an ordinary sperm-concentration check.',
 'Keep using contraception until your vasectomy service confirms clearance under its follow-up process. Use the semen test that service arranges and follow its exact instructions. An ordinary home fertility or sperm-concentration kit is not a substitute for post-vasectomy semen analysis and cannot give clearance.', ['S19'],[
  ['The follow-up has a specific job','A post-vasectomy semen test checks the result of the procedure under the service’s clearance criteria. The laboratory and clinical team decide what the finding means and whether another sample is needed. Do not treat the date of the operation, an elapsed period or an informal home check as permission to stop contraception.',['S19']],
  ['Use your service’s instructions','The service should explain when to provide the sample, how to collect it and how it reaches the correct laboratory. Use its own timetable and collection requirements. If you have lost the instructions or are unsure whether you have completed follow-up, contact the service. This guide intentionally does not replace that process with one universal collection rule.',[]],
  ['Why a retail fertility kit is different','A concentration-only fertility kit is designed to answer a different question. It does not apply the laboratory process needed for post-vasectomy clearance. A reassuring reading on that product does not establish that the operation has achieved clearance. The same limitation applies if someone else recommends a kit because it was useful in their own fertility assessment.',['S19']],
  ['If the sample or result is delayed','Contact the service responsible for your follow-up and ask whether the sample was received, when you should hear and whether any action is needed. Tell them if collection or transport did not follow the instructions. Continue contraception while awaiting their confirmation. Do not fill the gap with a different test and assume it answers the same question.',[]],
  ['Get a clear confirmation','Ask how clearance will be communicated and what you should do if another sample is requested. Keep that information with your procedure paperwork. A useful next step is to find the follow-up contact details before collecting anything. Ask Timber can explain why the tests differ, but cannot certify clearance, interpret your own report or tell you that contraception can stop.',[]]
 ],['D02','D03','C08'],'/shift-health/fertility'),
 G('D07','fertility','anabolic-steroids-and-fertility-support',
 'Past anabolic steroid use and fertility: asking for specialist help',
 'Prepare an honest treatment history and seek appropriate help without following a self-managed hormone recovery plan.',
 'Anabolic steroid use can affect sperm production and fertility. Tell the GP or fertility specialist what you have used and when, including any other hormones or supplements. Effects and recovery vary; a general guide cannot promise a timetable. Seek professional help instead of copying an online cycle, taper or recovery protocol.', ['S37'],[
  ['Say what happened without needing a perfect record','If you do not remember every product or date, explain what you do know. Bring labels, photographs or previous results if available. Say whether use is current or in the past and whether anything else was taken alongside it. You are asking for an assessment, not presenting a case that has to be complete before someone can help.',[]],
  ['Fertility is one of the possible effects','The NHS lists reduced sperm count and infertility among possible effects of anabolic steroid use, alongside wider physical and mental health harms. A clinician needs to consider the whole history. A testosterone reading alone cannot establish the state of sperm production or explain every symptom someone experiences after use.',['S37','S06']],
  ['Avoid a guaranteed recovery story','People online may describe what happened to them, but that does not set your own outcome or timeline. Ask the clinical team what assessment is needed and what uncertainty remains. Do not buy additional hormones or “post-cycle” products on the assumption that a standard combination will restore fertility. This page supplies no protocol or individual stopping advice.',[]],
  ['Prepare for coordinated care','Explain your family plans, current symptoms and previous investigations. Ask whether input from a fertility or hormone specialist is appropriate and who will coordinate it. Keep copies of results and letters so information can move between teams. If sleep, mood or day-to-day functioning are also affected, include those concerns in the conversation rather than restricting it to sperm count.',[]],
  ['One useful next step','Book a GP conversation and write a short account of use, symptoms and what you want help with. If you feel unwell or have urgent symptoms, seek medical help promptly rather than waiting for a fertility appointment. Ask Timber can help organise questions and explain general concepts; it cannot assess recovery, interpret personal hormone results or choose medicines to manage it.',[]]
 ],['D03','D05','T02'],'/shift-health/fertility'),
 G('D08','fertility','fertility-emotional-and-relationship-support',
 'When trying for a baby is affecting your head or relationship',
 'Practical ways to ask for support, share the load and make room for life beyond appointments and results.',
 'Fertility concerns can affect wellbeing and relationships, and support is a valid part of care. Ask your clinic or GP about counselling and other support. Difficulty coping is not proof that you caused a fertility problem, and being told to “just relax” is not a useful treatment plan.', ['S38'],[
  ['There is no required way to feel','You might feel anxious, angry, flat, hopeful or several things on the same day. People involved may respond differently. The HFEA describes counselling and other emotional support during fertility treatment. You do not need to wait until things are unbearable, or until you have a particular diagnosis, before saying that the process is difficult.',['S38']],
  ['Keep blame out of the conversation','The HFEA notes that evidence does not show stress determines the outcome of fertility treatment. That does not mean distress is unimportant; it deserves support in its own right. Avoid making calmness another task someone has to complete to deserve a family. A difficult week or an argument is not a personal explanation for an unsuccessful treatment.',['S38']],
  ['Make one manageable agreement','If a partner is involved, agree a time to discuss appointments and a time when you can leave the subject alone. Decide who keeps paperwork, takes notes or follows up a question. Either person can ask for a pause in a conversation. You may find it easier to begin with “What would help this week?” than trying to solve the whole uncertainty at once.',[]],
  ['Choose support that feels usable','Ask a clinic about qualified counselling, eligibility, cost and waiting times. A GP can also help you discuss the effect on your wellbeing. Peer support may offer understanding, but another person’s treatment story is not a forecast for your own. You can set boundaries around forums, social media and questions from family or colleagues.',['S38']],
  ['Bring the wider concern into care','At an appointment, you can say “This is affecting our relationship” or “I am struggling to cope with waiting.” Write it down if saying it aloud feels difficult. Ask what support is available alongside the medical assessment. Ask Timber can help frame that conversation and explain general information; it cannot replace counselling or assess an individual’s mental health.',[]]
 ],['D01','D04','D05'],'/shift-health/fertility')
);

expansionGuides.push(
 G('E03','sleep','sleep-routine-and-persistent-insomnia',
 'Struggling to sleep: a practical routine and when to get help',
 'Make an evening plan that fits your household and recognise when persistent sleep difficulty needs more than habit advice.',
 'A consistent routine and a comfortable sleep environment may help, but persistent insomnia deserves assessment. Speak to a GP if sleep problems have lasted months, changes have not helped or daily life is difficult. Treatment can include cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia; buying a sleep supplement is not the required next step.', ['S39','S45'],[
  ['Choose a realistic starting point','Begin with the part of the evening you can control. That might mean preparing tomorrow’s essentials earlier, putting the phone aside or setting a regular wind-down reminder. Keep the plan small enough to repeat on an ordinary work night. A difficult household schedule does not mean you have failed at sleep or should simply try harder.',[]],
  ['Give sleep a workable setting','NHS advice includes a regular routine and a bedroom that is quiet, dark and comfortable. Try a change that addresses your actual obstacle: an eye mask for light, reducing interruptions or making space for a calm activity before bed. Consider caffeine and alcohol as part of the pattern rather than focusing only on the bedroom.',['S41']],
  ['Avoid turning the night into a score','Notice whether the routine helps how you feel during the day. You do not have to produce a perfect night or a particular wearable score. If recording sleep helps, keep notes brief: bedtime, waking, work pattern and daytime impact. If detailed tracking adds pressure, take a simpler account to the healthcare professional instead.',[]],
  ['When habits are not enough','Long-lasting difficulty falling asleep or staying asleep can require a fuller assessment of causes and treatment. The NHS describes GP support and cognitive behavioural therapy. CBT-I is a structured approach to insomnia, not merely another list of bedtime tips. Ask what is available and how any existing conditions or medicines fit the assessment.',['S39','S45']],
  ['Keep other symptoms visible','Tell a GP about breathing pauses, gasping, pronounced daytime sleepiness or any change that worries you. Those concerns should not be hidden inside a general “bad sleeper” label. If sleepiness affects driving, do not drive and seek medical advice. Ask Timber can help organise questions and explain general options; it cannot diagnose insomnia or prescribe a sleep treatment.',['S07','S50']]
 ],['E01','E05','F09'],'/shift-health/sleep-apnoea'),
 G('E04','sleep','shift-work-and-unrefreshing-sleep',
 'Shift work, late nights and unrefreshing sleep',
 'A practical approach to sleep around changing work patterns, with fatigue, the commute and workplace support included.',
 'Shift work can make regular sleep difficult. Plan sleep around the actual rota, protect a workable rest period and discuss fatigue risks with your employer. Persistent unrefreshing sleep or severe daytime sleepiness still needs medical attention; it should not automatically be dismissed as the unavoidable price of working shifts.', ['S40','S39'],[
  ['Use the rota you actually have','Write down work hours, travel and caring commitments before planning sleep. A standard bedtime may be unrealistic across alternating shifts. HSE guidance encourages shift workers to identify a suitable sleep schedule and use practical changes that fit their circumstances. A short diary can show where the biggest loss of sleep opportunity happens.',['S40']],
  ['Protect the sleep period','For daytime sleep, consider light, noise, temperature and interruptions. Agree with people at home when you need quiet and when you are available. Prepare food and essentials ahead where that saves time after a shift. The point is to create a usable rest opportunity, not to add an elaborate routine to an already crowded day.',['S40']],
  ['Look at the whole pattern','Notice caffeine use, meals, recovery days and the journey home. If you test a change, choose one that you can assess over several relevant shifts. Explain your work pattern when seeking medical help or booking tests so the professional can give appropriate instructions. Do not force a day-worker timetable onto a night-work schedule without discussing the practical difficulty.',[]],
  ['Raise fatigue as a work issue','Tell an appropriate manager or occupational health team if tiredness affects concentration or safe work. Ask about the relevant fatigue procedures and what support or adjustments can be considered. You should not have to solve an unsafe pattern solely by buying supplements. HSE guidance treats shift-related fatigue as a practical health and safety concern.',['S40']],
  ['Know when to seek more help','Contact a GP if sleep problems persist, affect daily life or come with breathing pauses or other worrying symptoms. Do not drive when sleepiness makes it unsafe; arrange another way home and seek medical advice. A useful next step is to bring a brief rota-and-sleep record to that discussion. Ask Timber cannot assess fitness to drive or diagnose a sleep disorder.',['S39','S50']]
 ],['E03','E05','E08'],'/shift-health/sleep-apnoea'),
 G('E05','sleep','caffeine-alcohol-and-sleep',
 'Caffeine, alcohol and sleep: what is worth changing?',
 'Notice the pattern, try a manageable change and keep the focus on how you feel rather than a perfect set of rules.',
 'Caffeine can make it harder to settle, and alcohol can disrupt sleep even if it initially makes you feel sleepy. Look at timing and the whole pattern, then try a realistic change. If sleep problems persist or affect daily life, ask a GP rather than relying on another drink or supplement.', ['S39','S41'],[
  ['Start with what you actually drink','For a few ordinary days, note coffee, tea, energy drinks, pre-workout products and alcohol, including roughly when you have them. Include drinks during night shifts and days off. You do not need a detailed nutrition spreadsheet. The aim is to spot a pattern you can discuss or change, not to grade yourself.',[]],
  ['Move the useful decision earlier','NHS sleep advice identifies caffeine and alcohol among things that can disturb sleep. If a late caffeinated drink is part of your routine, consider trying it earlier or choosing a caffeine-free alternative. Match the change to your intended sleep period, particularly with shifts. Do not assume a product described as “natural energy” is caffeine-free; check the label.',['S39','S41']],
  ['Separate feeling sleepy from sleeping well','An alcoholic drink may be associated with winding down, but it is not a treatment for a sleep problem. Consider whether you can keep the part of the evening you enjoy while changing the drink or timing. If you are concerned about your alcohol use or find changing it difficult, ask a GP or appropriate support service for help.',['S39']],
  ['Choose one change you can judge','For example, replace the last caffeinated drink of the day while keeping the rest of the evening broadly similar. Notice sleep and daytime functioning rather than chasing one wearable score. If work, pain, stress or caring responsibilities are the main obstacle, acknowledge that too. A drink change cannot solve every reason someone is awake.',[]],
  ['Do not hide a persistent problem','If difficulty sleeping has continued for months, affects daily life or comes with pronounced daytime sleepiness, arrange medical advice. Mention snoring and breathing pauses if present. Ask Timber can help you describe a pattern or understand general sleep information, but cannot decide the cause. Persistent insomnia may need a structured assessment and treatment rather than a growing collection of sleep products.',['S39','S07']]
 ],['E03','E04','F09'],'/shift-health/sleep-apnoea'),
 G('E06','sleep','cpap-comfort-routine-and-support',
 'Living with CPAP: comfort, routine and who can help',
 'Practical support around prescribed sleep treatment, including mask problems, equipment care and preparing for a clinic review.',
 'If CPAP is uncomfortable or difficult to use, contact the sleep service that manages it. Mask fit, leaks, dryness and routine problems may need support. Follow the instructions for your equipment and do not change prescribed pressure settings yourself. Persistent sleepiness needs review, especially if driving or work safety is affected.', ['S49','S50'],[
  ['Name the specific difficulty','Write down what makes use difficult: a leaking mask, sore skin, a dry mouth, noise or feeling unsettled by the equipment. Note when it happens and what the clinic has already advised. This gives the team a clearer starting point than “I cannot get on with it”. You are allowed to ask for help before the next routine appointment.',[]],
  ['Ask about mask fit','NHS CPAP support information describes leaks and poor fit among practical problems that may need review. Tightening a mask more and more is not a universal solution. Ask the responsible team to check the fit and explain suitable options for your equipment. Do not block vents or make homemade changes to the mask or tubing.',['S49']],
  ['Use the equipment’s own care instructions','Keep the manufacturer’s and service’s instructions available for cleaning, drying and checking parts. Products and components differ, so do not assume a social-media cleaning method is suitable. Ask how to request replacement parts and what to do if something is damaged. Know the service contact details before a problem leaves you unable to use the equipment.',[]],
  ['Plan around ordinary life','Set aside space for the equipment and the small preparation tasks that make bedtime easier. Before travel, ask the service and relevant travel provider about practical arrangements for your particular device. Bring your questions about dryness, comfort or routine to the team rather than buying an accessory without checking compatibility. Pressure and treatment decisions belong with the clinical service.',[]],
  ['Review how you feel, not just the machine','Tell the sleep service if you remain very sleepy or do not feel better. A screen reading alone does not establish fitness to drive. If sleepiness affects safe driving, do not drive and seek medical advice; follow the applicable DVLA rules. Ask Timber can help organise a list of difficulties for the clinic, but cannot interpret device data or adjust treatment.',['S49','S50']]
 ],['E01','E02','E08'],'/shift-health/sleep-apnoea'),
 G('E07','sleep','sleep-trackers-and-wearable-limits',
 'Sleep trackers and wearables: useful clues, clear limits',
 'Understand what a trend or alert can contribute and what to ask about the exact device, function and clinical pathway.',
 'A tracker can help record patterns, but a sleep score is not a complete clinical assessment. Capabilities differ between devices and functions, including some functions intended for medical use. Check the evidence and instructions for the exact feature. Discuss symptoms or concerning alerts with a healthcare professional; a reassuring score should not dismiss them.', ['S47','S07'],[
  ['Know what is being estimated','Consumer devices can use movement, pulse-related signals and algorithms to estimate sleep. A score may be specific to that product, and the label “deep sleep” does not tell you how it was established. The AASM’s technology framework distinguishes sensors, derived outputs, intended functions and validation; those distinctions are more useful than treating all wearables as equivalent.',['S47']],
  ['Separate the function from the device','Ask what the particular feature is intended to do, for whom and under which instructions. Evidence for one model or function does not automatically validate every output of the brand. Technology changes, so an old blanket claim that no wearable has a medical role is unhelpful. Equally, one validated feature does not turn every sleep score into a diagnosis.',['S47']],
  ['Use a pattern to support a conversation','If tracking helps, take a brief summary of bedtimes, waking and daytime symptoms to an appointment. Explain how the device was worn and whether an alert prompted concern. You do not need to export months of data before asking for help. A description of falling asleep at work or a partner noticing breathing pauses is valuable information in its own right.',[]],
  ['Do not let a score overrule symptoms','Tell a GP about gasping, breathing pauses, loud snoring or pronounced daytime sleepiness even if the app looks reassuring. A clinician may arrange a sleep assessment. Ask what further information is needed rather than repeatedly changing devices to find a more comfortable score. Ask Timber cannot diagnose apnoea or assess treatment from a screenshot.',['S07']],
  ['Decide whether tracking is helping you','Before buying or continuing a subscription, ask what decision the information will support, what it costs and how the provider handles your data. If nightly checking adds worry without a useful next step, a simpler diary may be enough for the conversation. Bring questions about alerts to the healthcare professional or device support team appropriate to the concern.',[]]
 ],['E01','E02','E03'],'/shift-health/sleep-apnoea'),
 G('E08','sleep','daytime-sleepiness-driving-and-work',
 'Daytime sleepiness, driving and safety at work',
 'Keep immediate safety and the route to assessment visible before considering a sleep test or product.',
 'Do not drive when sleepiness affects your ability to drive safely. If already driving, stop somewhere safe and arrange a safe alternative. Seek medical advice about excessive sleepiness and follow the relevant DVLA requirements. At work, raise fatigue that affects safe tasks through the appropriate manager or occupational health route.', ['S50','S40'],[
  ['Act on the immediate risk','Fighting to stay awake is a reason to stop a safety-critical activity, not to test your willpower. Do not begin or continue a journey when you cannot stay alert. If you are already driving, find a safe place to stop and arrange an alternative. Buying a sleep test, opening a window or obtaining a reassuring wearable score cannot establish fitness to drive.',['S50']],
  ['Ask for an assessment','Tell a GP about the sleepiness, when it happens and any episodes of nodding off. Mention breathing pauses, snoring, work patterns and medicines. Excessive sleepiness can have different causes, so do not assume either sleep apnoea or a lack of effort. Explain the effect on driving or work clearly rather than using only the word “tired”.',['S07','S08']],
  ['Check the applicable driving rules','DVLA guidance sets different requirements depending on the condition and licence group. Confirmed moderate or severe obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome with excessive sleepiness requires notification; other sleep conditions have their own rules. Ask the doctor and use the current DVLA guidance for your circumstances. This guide cannot decide when you may resume driving or certify symptom control.',['S50','S08']],
  ['Include work and the commute','Raise fatigue affecting safe work with an appropriate manager or occupational health team and follow the workplace procedure. Consider the journey home as part of the problem, especially after a night shift. HSE guidance covers practical fatigue concerns for shift workers. A personal sleep routine may help, but it does not replace an appropriate response to an unsafe work situation.',['S40']],
  ['Prepare a short account','Write down recent examples, your work pattern and any medicines to discuss. Ask who will arrange assessment, how to seek help while waiting and what restrictions apply to your activities. Do not wait for a commercial booking before raising a safety concern. Ask Timber can explain general pathways and help organise questions; assessment and fitness decisions require the responsible professional.',[]]
 ],['E01','E04','E06'],'/shift-health/sleep-apnoea')
);

expansionGuides.push(
 G('F04','supplements','creatine-monohydrate-evidence-and-purpose',
 'Creatine monohydrate: purpose, evidence and realistic expectations',
 'Understand its specific place in training and the questions to ask before deciding whether it is useful for you.',
 'Creatine monohydrate has evidence for some repeated, short, high-intensity exercise, such as aspects of strength training. It is not a weight-loss treatment or a guarantee of muscle gain. Whether it has a useful place depends on your activity, expectations and suitability; a general guide cannot choose a personal dose.', ['S43'],[
  ['Be clear about the purpose','Creatine helps the body’s energy supply during certain demanding efforts. The NIH evidence summary describes benefits for some repeated high-intensity activities, with less value for endurance exercise. That is a specific purpose. It should not be stretched into a promise to fix everyday tiredness, replace training or treat low testosterone.',['S43']],
  ['Start with the activity','Ask what you actually want the supplement to help with. If the goal is beginning a manageable exercise routine, access, confidence and a repeatable plan may be more useful first decisions. If you already train, ask how you will judge whether a product adds anything. An impressive before-and-after photograph does not show what caused the change.',[]],
  ['Understand the scale conversation','Creatine can increase body weight through water retention. A rise on the scale is not automatically an increase in body fat, nor proof of new muscle. If you are monitoring weight for another reason, discuss the context with the relevant professional. Avoid promising a particular amount of strength, muscle or weight change to every user.',['S43']],
  ['Check the actual product','Read whether the product is creatine monohydrate alone or a blend containing caffeine, vitamins or other ingredients. Compare the labelled daily serving, warnings, price and overlap with anything else you use. Evidence about an ingredient does not validate every claim made for a mixed product. You should be able to explain the intended purpose without repeating a marketing slogan.',[]],
  ['Check suitability before a decision','Ask a pharmacist or clinician if you take medicines, have a health condition or are unsure whether it is suitable. Tell them what is in the exact product. Do not use it as a way to postpone assessment of unexplained weakness or tiredness. Ask Timber can explain the evidence generally and help compare labels; it cannot choose a treatment or individual supplement regimen.',['S09']]
 ],['F01','F02','F03'],'/shift-health/supplements-nutrition'),
 G('F05','supplements','vitamin-d-uk-seasonal-advice',
 'Vitamin D: UK seasonal advice and avoiding overlap',
 'Read the general NHS advice, understand the units and separate an everyday supplement decision from deficiency treatment.',
 'NHS advice is for adults to consider a daily supplement containing 10 micrograms of vitamin D in autumn and winter. Some people should consider it throughout the year because of limited sun exposure or other risk factors. This is general advice, not a personal deficiency-treatment dose; follow your clinician’s advice if you have a prescribed plan.', ['S30'],[
  ['Why the seasons matter','In the UK, sunlight does not support enough vitamin D production during the colder part of the year. NHS advice therefore includes considering a supplement in autumn and winter. People with little sun exposure and people with dark skin may need to consider year-round supplementation. Read the NHS guidance for the circumstances it describes.',['S30']],
  ['Read the unit as well as the number','Vitamin D labels may use micrograms, written mcg or µg, or International Units, written IU. Ten micrograms equals 400 IU. A milligram is a different unit. Before comparing products, look at the amount in the complete labelled daily serving, not just a large number on the front of the pack.',['S30']],
  ['Check for overlap','Make a short list of multivitamins, bone-health products and other supplements you already take. Vitamin D may appear in more than one. Bring the labels to a pharmacist if the combined amount is unclear. Buying a second product is not the same as identifying a second need, and a more concentrated product is not automatically a better everyday choice.',[]],
  ['Separate general advice from treatment','A clinician may prescribe a different plan for a diagnosed deficiency or particular medical circumstances. Do not replace that with a general guide, add extra products without checking, or copy someone else’s course. If you have a condition or treatment that affects supplement suitability, ask the responsible professional. More vitamin D is not automatically safer.',['S30']],
  ['Make the next step proportionate','Read the current NHS page and the label of anything you already use. If you are uncertain, ask a pharmacist to help you understand the general advice and overlap. If symptoms are the concern, describe them to a GP rather than assuming vitamin D is the explanation. Ask Timber cannot diagnose deficiency, interpret a personal level or set a replacement regimen.',[]]
 ],['B08','F01','F02'],'/shift-health/supplements-nutrition'),
 G('F06','supplements','iron-supplements-and-unexplained-tiredness',
 'Iron supplements: why tiredness alone is not a reason to start',
 'Understand why identifying the cause matters, what a result can contribute and how to avoid a reflex purchase.',
 'Tiredness has many possible causes and does not by itself establish iron deficiency. Iron treatment should follow an appropriate assessment of need and cause. Ask a GP about persistent unexplained tiredness and a pharmacist or clinician about supplement suitability; do not use a home indicator as an automatic instruction to start iron.', ['S01','S24'],[
  ['Begin with the problem you want explained','Write down how long you have felt tired, what has changed in daily life and any other symptoms. Tell the GP about medicines and supplements. Iron deficiency is one possible explanation, but assuming it first can steer attention away from the actual cause. You do not need to buy a kit or start a supplement before asking for an assessment.',['S01']],
  ['Testing needs context','A full blood count and ferritin contribute different information. The clinical question is not simply whether a number is flagged: it includes whether deficiency is present and why. Ferritin can also be influenced by inflammation. Ask the professional to explain the whole report rather than comparing one number with a threshold from another laboratory.',['S24','S25']],
  ['If deficiency is diagnosed','Ask about treatment, investigation of the cause and how response will be checked. Tell the clinician about any side effects or difficulty following the plan. Do not assume a product sold for general wellbeing supplies the treatment you need. The NHS describes iron deficiency anaemia care as more than replacing a nutrient without understanding why it became low.',['S24']],
  ['Keep supplements safe','Iron products can cause unwanted effects and can be dangerous if too much is taken, particularly by children. Keep them securely out of children’s reach. Check for iron in combination products and tell the pharmacist about everything you take. Do not copy a friend’s prescription or double up because a label calls a product “gentle” or “natural”.',['S48']],
  ['Avoid an automatic checkout step','A useful test service should explain results and follow-up, not leave you to infer a product choice from a coloured flag. Ask what evidence establishes the need and who is responsible for the clinical advice. Ask Timber can explain blood-count and ferritin terminology and help prepare questions. It cannot diagnose iron deficiency or choose your personal dose or treatment duration.',[]]
 ],['B05','B01','F02'],'/shift-health/supplements-nutrition'),
 G('F07','supplements','electrolytes-and-everyday-hydration',
 'Electrolytes and hydration: do you need more than a drink?',
 'Separate everyday drinking, exercise products and oral rehydration advice for illness.',
 'For an ordinary day, regular fluids are a sensible starting point; an electrolyte product is not automatically necessary. Illness, heat, activity and individual health circumstances can change what is appropriate. Oral rehydration solutions used for fluid loss from vomiting or diarrhoea have a different purpose from a general sports or wellness drink.', ['S42'],[
  ['Start with the situation','Are you forgetting to drink during a workday, exercising in heat or struggling to keep fluids down during illness? Those are different problems. The NHS describes thirst, darker urine and passing urine less often among signs of dehydration. Use the circumstances and symptoms to decide whether you need practical drinking support or medical advice.',['S42']],
  ['Make ordinary drinking easier','Put a drink where it is accessible, plan breaks where possible and notice which parts of the day make drinking difficult. If nausea makes drinking uncomfortable, small sips may be easier. A branded powder does not solve lack of access to a drink or a toilet break. Start by addressing the obstacle you can identify.',['S42']],
  ['Check what an exercise product adds','Read the ingredients, serving size, sugar, caffeine and cost. Ask which feature is relevant to your activity rather than assuming every workout requires electrolyte replacement. Compare a product with the ordinary food and drinks already in your routine. If a health condition or medicine affects fluid advice, discuss suitability with the responsible professional before using a general sports recommendation.',[]],
  ['Illness is a different context','For vomiting or diarrhoea, a pharmacist can advise about an appropriate oral rehydration solution. Follow the named product’s preparation instructions; a sports drink is not automatically an equivalent. Seek medical advice if you are concerned, symptoms persist or you cannot manage fluids. Do not keep buying drinks as a substitute for assessment when you are becoming more unwell.',['S42']],
  ['Keep treatment questions with your clinician','If you are taking a medicine and new symptoms are making eating or drinking difficult, contact the prescriber or appropriate medical service. Do not assume an electrolyte mix makes the problem safe to ignore or change the medicine yourself. Ask Timber can explain the general difference between products and help organise questions, but cannot assess dehydration or choose a personal replacement regimen.',[]]
 ],['F01','F02','B07'],'/shift-health/supplements-nutrition'),
 G('F08','supplements','collagen-supplements-evidence',
 'Collagen supplements: what does the evidence actually support?',
 'Look beyond before-and-after claims and separate skin research, joint claims and evidence for a particular product.',
 'Collagen claims need scrutiny of the outcome, study quality and exact product. A 2025 skin-ageing review found apparent pooled benefits, but not in its higher-quality or non-industry-funded study subgroups. That weakens a confident skin promise. It does not establish whether every collagen product works or settle every question about joints.', ['S44'],[
  ['Ask which claim is being made','Skin hydration, wrinkles, joint pain and weight change are different outcomes. A study about one cannot automatically support the others. Before comparing brands, write the claim in plain language and ask what evidence would actually show it. General information about collagen’s role in the body is not proof that swallowing a particular powder delivers that advertised result.',[]],
  ['Read the important qualification','The 2025 systematic review included randomised studies of collagen supplements and skin ageing. Its overall pooled analysis looked favourable, but the findings did not hold in the higher-quality or non-industry-funded subgroups. Those differences matter when judging confidence. A sales page that reports only the favourable combined result leaves out an important part of the evidence.',['S44']],
  ['Do not stretch the conclusion','The review concerned particular skin outcomes and included particular studies. It is not a product test of every current brand and does not resolve all claims about joints. Treat that as a reason for specific, cautious questions. Ask for relevant research on the exact outcome and product rather than accepting either a universal promise or a universal dismissal.',[]],
  ['Read the whole label','Check the source of the collagen, ingredients, allergens, serving size and any added vitamins or other supplements. Calculate the cost of the labelled serving over the period you are considering. If you take medicines or have a condition, ask about suitability. An expensive blend should be able to justify its practical purpose without leaning on a scientific-sounding ingredient list.',['S09']],
  ['You can decide it is not useful','A supplement does not have to earn a place in every routine. Consider what you want to achieve, how certain the evidence is and what else you could do with that budget. Seek proper assessment for persistent joint symptoms or other health concerns. Ask Timber can help examine a claim or label; it cannot promise an individual result or choose a treatment.',[]]
 ],['F01','F02','F03'],'/shift-health/supplements-nutrition'),
 G('F09','supplements','magnesium-and-sleep-supplement-claims',
 'Magnesium and sleep supplements: checking the promises',
 'Separate a nutrient’s role from evidence that a supplement treats insomnia, and keep a useful sleep assessment in view.',
 'Evidence for magnesium supplements improving insomnia is limited and uncertain. A nutrient being important to the body does not prove that taking extra will solve sleep problems. Persistent insomnia deserves an appropriate assessment; ask about established options such as cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia rather than assuming a sleep blend is necessary.', ['S45'],[
  ['Read the claim carefully','“Supports normal function” and “treats insomnia” are different kinds of claim. Check what the seller is actually promising and whether the research studies that outcome. A calm-looking label or a list of familiar nutrients is not evidence that the finished blend helps someone stay asleep or addresses the cause of their sleep difficulty.',[]],
  ['What the evidence summary says','NCCIH describes limited research on magnesium for insomnia and mixed or low-quality evidence in reviews. That does not establish a reliable benefit for everyone, a best form or an individual dose. Its summary also distinguishes the stronger evidence for structured insomnia treatment from the uncertainty surrounding many complementary approaches.',['S45']],
  ['Separate deficiency from a general sleep claim','If a clinician is investigating or treating a deficiency, that is a particular care question. It is different from recommending magnesium to everyone who sleeps badly. Do not infer a deficiency from a restless night, an online symptom list or an influencer’s personal response. Explain persistent symptoms to the appropriate healthcare professional rather than deciding on the nutrient first.',[]],
  ['Check the entire blend','Read every ingredient, not just magnesium, and look for overlap with other products. Ask a pharmacist or clinician about suitability with medicines and health conditions. More ingredients do not establish more benefit. Compare the cost and the promise with what is known, and do not increase the serving because a first attempt did not produce the night you hoped for.',['S09']],
  ['Keep the next step useful','Take a brief account of sleep, work patterns and daytime impact to a GP if the problem persists or disrupts daily life. Mention breathing pauses, snoring and pronounced sleepiness. Ask what assessment or CBT-I support is available. Ask Timber can help explain general evidence and prepare questions; it cannot diagnose deficiency, choose a personal supplement dose or replace a sleep assessment.',['S39','S07']]
 ],['E03','E07','F02'],'/shift-health/supplements-nutrition'),
 G('F10','supplements','testosterone-booster-claims-and-evidence',
 'Testosterone boosters: claims, evidence and questions to ask',
 'Understand why a supplement claim, a hormone result and prescribed treatment are different things.',
 'A product marketed as a “testosterone booster” is not equivalent to prescribed TRT or fertility treatment. A 2023 systematic review found that most proposed boosters studied did not increase total testosterone; some findings were limited to particular populations. That does not establish that a retail blend treats symptoms or a diagnosed deficiency.', ['S46'],[
  ['Ask what “boost” is supposed to mean','The word can refer to a laboratory measurement, a feeling of energy, gym performance or sexual wellbeing. Those are different claims. Ask which outcome was studied, how it was measured and whether the result mattered to participants. A higher number, where shown, is not automatically evidence of better health or a treatment for someone’s symptoms.',[]],
  ['Look at the study, not just the ingredient','The systematic review assessed studies of proposed boosters and found that most did not raise total testosterone. Some effects depended on the population studied. Evidence about an isolated ingredient in one group does not establish benefit from a particular mixture, serving or brand. A claim should reflect those limits rather than turning a possible signal into a guarantee.',['S46']],
  ['Keep assessment separate from marketing','Symptoms associated with low testosterone can have other explanations. Appropriate diagnosis considers symptoms and consistently low measurements, with further assessment where needed. A short questionnaire, gym plateau or retail kit result cannot decide that a person needs treatment. Ask a healthcare professional about persistent concerns rather than using a booster as an informal diagnostic trial.',['S03','S02']],
  ['Do not confuse it with fertility care','A product promoted for male vitality does not establish fertility benefit. Prescribed testosterone also has important fertility implications, so “more testosterone” is not a safe universal family-planning goal. Tell the clinician about all supplements and hormones you use, including products bought online. Do not combine them into a self-managed hormone or recovery plan.',['S03']],
  ['A useful buying question','Ask for evidence about the finished product, the relevant outcome and people with circumstances like yours. Check all ingredients, warnings, overlap and cost. You can decide a product has not earned a place. Ask Timber can help examine general evidence or explain TRT terminology; it cannot diagnose low testosterone, interpret your result or select a personal hormone or supplement regimen.',[]]
 ],['T01','T02','F02'],'/shift-health/supplements-nutrition')
);

const questions=[
 ['T05','What follow-up should TRT include?','Ask your responsible prescriber who arranges monitoring, reviews results and handles problems between appointments.'],
 ['T08','Do erection problems or low sex drive always mean low testosterone?','Describe the change to a GP; recurring erection problems can also be discussed with a sexual health service.'],
 ['B03','What is the difference between HbA1c and blood glucose?','Ask which measurement is being arranged, what preparation applies and who explains the result.'],
 ['B05','What is the difference between ferritin and a full blood count?','Take the complete report to the clinician who arranged it and ask what the findings and cause mean for your care.'],
 ['B06','Does a home TSH test check my whole thyroid?','Read exactly what the kit measures and discuss symptoms or an unexpected finding with a healthcare professional.'],
 ['B07','What can liver and kidney blood tests tell me?','Ask the professional arranging testing to explain the panel, findings and any follow-up.'],
 ['B08','Does everyone need vitamin D or B12 blood tests?','Ask what clinical question a proposed test answers and how the result would change care.'],
 ['C03','How accurate are home tests?','Ask for evidence about the exact kit and intended use, and check who handles unclear results.'],
 ['C04','Do I need to fast or stop medicines before a blood test?','Follow the provider’s exact instructions and ask before changing prescribed medicines.'],
 ['C05','Are finger-prick, upper-arm and venous blood samples interchangeable?','Confirm the laboratory’s accepted collection method for the particular panel and its current availability.'],
 ['C06','Can a negative FIT result rule out bowel cancer?','Speak to a GP about bowel symptoms even after a reassuring test; use urgent services for the warning symptoms in the guide.'],
 ['C07','Is a urine albumin test a complete kidney check?','Ask how the particular urine test fits the wider assessment and who explains the findings.'],
 ['C08','Does an invalid home test mean negative?','Check the kit instructions and contact its provider; do not delay medical advice for symptoms.'],
 ['D03','What does a full semen analysis measure?','Use the laboratory’s collection instructions and ask the clinical team to explain the complete report.'],
 ['D04','Can lifestyle changes guarantee better fertility?','Make realistic changes alongside the appropriate assessment; do not wait for a perfect routine before asking for help.'],
 ['D05','Should I stop a medicine if I am worried about fertility?','Bring a complete medicines, hormones and supplements list to your prescriber or fertility team.'],
 ['D06','Can a home sperm kit give clearance after a vasectomy?','Follow your vasectomy service’s semen-testing process and keep using contraception until it confirms clearance.'],
 ['D07','Can past anabolic steroid use affect fertility?','Tell a GP what you used and when, and ask about appropriate specialist assessment.'],
 ['D08','Where can I get emotional support during fertility assessment?','Ask your clinic or GP about counselling and support, including access, cost and waiting times.'],
 ['E03','When should I get help for insomnia?','Take a brief account of your sleep and daytime impact to a GP and ask what assessment or CBT-I support is available.'],
 ['E04','How can I manage sleep around shift work?','Bring your actual rota and sleep pattern to the discussion and raise fatigue affecting safe work.'],
 ['E05','Can caffeine and alcohol affect sleep?','Try one manageable change in timing or choice and seek medical advice for a persistent sleep problem.'],
 ['E06','What should I do if my CPAP mask leaks or feels uncomfortable?','Contact your own sleep service with the specific difficulty; do not alter prescribed pressure settings yourself.'],
 ['E07','Can a sleep tracker diagnose a sleep disorder?','Check the exact device function and discuss symptoms or concerning alerts with a healthcare professional.'],
 ['E08','Can I drive if I am struggling to stay awake?','Stop safely if already driving, arrange a safe alternative and seek medical advice about excessive sleepiness.'],
 ['F04','What is creatine monohydrate useful for?','Start with your activity and purpose, read the whole label and ask about suitability where needed.'],
 ['F05','What is the NHS vitamin D advice for UK adults?','Check the current NHS guidance and supplements you already use; ask a pharmacist about overlap.'],
 ['F06','Should I take iron because I feel tired?','Discuss persistent unexplained tiredness with a GP and ask about the cause before choosing a supplement.'],
 ['F07','Do I need electrolyte supplements every day?','Match the next step to the situation; ask a pharmacist about oral rehydration for vomiting or diarrhoea.'],
 ['F08','Do collagen supplements have strong evidence for skin ageing?','Ask what the exact product and outcome evidence shows, including study quality and funding.'],
 ['F09','Is magnesium a proven treatment for insomnia?','Discuss persistent sleep difficulty with a GP and ask about established insomnia support.'],
 ['F10','Are testosterone booster supplements the same as TRT?','Seek an appropriate assessment for persistent symptoms and check claims about the exact finished product.']
];
export const expansionAnswers=questions.map(([id,question,next])=>{
 const g=expansionGuides.find(g=>g.id===id);
 return {id,question,answer:g.answer,sources:g.answerSources,next,path:g.path,
  status:'source_checked',eligible_for_retrieval:true};
});
expansionAnswers.push(
 {id:'T03',question:'Why might testosterone blood tests need repeating?',
  answer:'A diagnosis considers symptoms alongside consistently low testosterone measurements, not one isolated result. A clinician may repeat a correctly timed test and assess total testosterone, free testosterone or related measurements where appropriate. Follow the instructions for your own test; a number alone does not decide whether TRT is suitable.',
  sources:['S03','S20'],next:'Ask the clinician which tests are needed, how to prepare and who will explain the results.',path:'/shift-health/testosterone-blood-tests'},
 {id:'T04',question:'How are gels and injections chosen for TRT?',
  answer:'A responsible clinician assesses whether TRT is appropriate, explains expected benefits and risks, and discusses suitable formulations. Gels and injections have different practical considerations. The choice and dose depend on individual assessment and monitoring; Ask Timber cannot select them for you.',
  sources:['S20'],next:'Read the existing TRT guide and ask the prescriber to explain the options and follow-up.',path:'/shift-health/trt-explained'},
 {id:'T06',question:'What should I check when comparing private TRT costs?',
  answer:'Ask for the total care costs: initial assessment, blood tests, medication, collection or delivery fees and ongoing reviews. Check what is included, what can cost extra and how cancellation or transfer works. A low advertised prescription price does not explain the whole care arrangement.',
  sources:[],next:'Request a current written breakdown from the provider; this answer does not quote or verify a live price.',path:'/shift-health/trt-costs-uk'},
 {id:'T07',question:'How are weight and testosterone related?',
  answer:'Body weight and health can be relevant when assessing low testosterone, but the relationship does not diagnose an individual or promise that weight change will resolve symptoms. A clinician considers symptoms, correctly interpreted tests and possible causes together. Do not assume weight or testosterone alone explains persistent tiredness.',
  sources:['S20','S02'],next:'Use the existing guide as background and discuss persistent symptoms with a healthcare professional.',path:'/articles/testosterone-weight-men'},
 {id:'B04',question:'What do total, HDL, LDL and triglycerides mean on a cholesterol report?',
  answer:'A lipid profile includes different blood fats. Total cholesterol is an overall measure; HDL and LDL are different cholesterol-carrying particles, and triglycerides are another type of blood fat. A clinician considers the profile alongside wider cardiovascular risk. A total-cholesterol-only home indicator does not provide the whole assessment.',
  sources:['S15'],next:'Ask the professional arranging testing to explain the full report and any appropriate next step.',path:'/articles/cholesterol-men'},
 {id:'F03',question:'Do I need protein powder or bars to get enough protein?',
  answer:'Protein foods can form part of ordinary meals; a powder or bar is a convenience option, not an automatic requirement. Consider what you already eat, the practical gap, ingredients, allergens and cost. If a condition or treatment affects eating or nutritional needs, ask an appropriate healthcare professional for individual advice.',
  sources:['S17','S43'],next:'Read the existing protein guide and compare practical food options before deciding whether a product is useful.',path:'/articles/protein-for-men-weight-management'}
);
